# Dev container secrets — copy to ~/.config/devcontainer/secrets and chmod 600
#
# Two-tier system:
#   Common  → ~/.config/devcontainer/secrets                          (shared across all projects)
#   Project → ~/.config/devcontainer/secrets.d/<DEVCONTAINER_PROJECT>  (per-project overrides)
#
# The value in secrets.d/<project> wins when the same key appears in both files.
# Both files use literal, single-line KEY=value. Spaces and additional '=' are
# allowed after the first '='; no shell expansion or command substitution runs.
# A leading 'export' and matching whole-value quotes are tolerated, but neither
# is needed. Invalid names or unterminated quotes fail container creation.
#
# The host initializeCommand validates these files and writes a mode-0600 Docker
# env file that is passed to the container with --env-file, so PID 1 and every
# process below it (shells, lifecycle hooks, the editor extension host, MCP
# subprocesses) inherit the values. A key added after the container was created
# is picked up only on the next rebuild/restart.
#
# How keys persist across rebuilds, and device-auth vs API-key auth: see
# AUTH-PERSISTENCE.md in this directory.

# ── GitHub push auth (credential routing by org) ─────────────────────────────
# git push routes to the right token based on the repo's GitHub org — see
# AUTH-PERSISTENCE.md "GitHub push auth". For each org you push to, define a PAT
# env var here; the router resolves which one per repo:
#   1. an org→var line in the host map file (see below), else
#   2. the <ORG>_GITHUB_TOKEN naming convention (org upper-cased, non-alnum → _), else
#   3. GITHUB_TOKEN (fallback).
# Org names are NOT stored in git — the map lives only on the host:
#   ~/.config/devcontainer/github-token-map   (one `org=ENV_VAR_NAME` per line)

# GITHUB_TOKEN=                 # default / fallback (personal; read-only on org repos)
# <ORG>_GITHUB_TOKEN=           # one per org with push rights; name per convention or map

# ── Git identity (common — shared across projects) ───────────────────────────
#
# ~/.gitconfig is on the container's ephemeral layer (wiped on rebuild), so git
# identity is re-derived at boot: on-create.sh writes these to the global
# gitconfig every create. Without them `git commit` fails with "Author identity
# unknown". Same values you'd put in `git config --global user.name/.email`.
# GIT_USER_NAME=Your Name
# GIT_USER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# ── AI tools (common — shared across projects) ───────────────────────────────

# Claude Code long-lived login token (subscription-backed). Generate once with
# `claude setup-token`; it does NOT refresh to disk, so setting it here persists
# the Claude Code login across every rebuild AND every worktree (env-secret
# mechanism — see AUTH-PERSISTENCE.md). Distinct from ANTHROPIC_API_KEY below,
# which is for raw Claude API usage outside Claude Code.
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Context7 MCP server (https://context7.com)
CONTEXT7_API_KEY=

# Gemini API key — the Gemini CLI's auth in this container (API-key mode).
# Remove it if you switch the CLI to "Login with Google"; the key takes precedence.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Anthropic API key (for Claude API usage outside Claude Code)
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# OpenAI API key. Leave UNSET if you use `codex login` — the key shadows the
# device login (see AUTH-PERSISTENCE.md "Per-tool auth setup").
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# capability:start codex_cloud
# ── Codex Cloud (hosted agent environment) ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Codex Cloud has its own secret store and exposes it to the setup and
# maintenance phases only; the values are stripped before the agent phase runs.
# `.codex/cloud/bootstrap.sh` therefore copies a fixed allow-list of generic
# names out of that phase into a mode-0600 file which the persisted cloud
# environment sources. Existing entries are upserted, never rewritten, and no
# value is ever echoed to a log.
#
# Allow-listed names (`secret_allow_list` in `.codex/cloud/contract.toml`):
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   CONTEXT7_API_KEY
#   GEMINI_API_KEY
#   GH_TOKEN
#   GIT_USER_EMAIL
#   GIT_USER_NAME
#   OPENAI_API_KEY
#   OPENROUTER_API_KEY
#
# To bridge one more variable without editing the contract, set
# CODEX_CLOUD_PERSIST_EXTRA_ENV in the hosted environment to a space- or
# comma-separated list of additional variable names.
#
# Deployment and production credentials are deliberately excluded. Cloud tasks
# never deploy, never push, and never run host orchestration, so those secrets
# stay in GitHub Actions where the deployment jobs live.
# capability:end codex_cloud

# capability:start sentry
# ── Telemetry (error reporting and source-map upload) ────────────────────────
#
# SENTRY_DSN is a PUBLIC value. It ships inside the client bundle, so anyone who
# loads the application already has it; the reference implementation passes it
# as a repository VARIABLE rather than a secret for exactly that reason. Putting
# it here is fine, and `external-writes.json` refuses to let the variable that
# carries it be named like a credential.
# SENTRY_DSN=
#
# The upload token is the opposite, and it is deliberately NOT listed here.
# `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` authorizes a remote write — a release, a deploy marker, a
# source-map upload — and it belongs to the CI step that performs one, in that
# step's own `env:`, environment-scoped. It has no use inside the development
# container: nothing here deploys.
#
# That separation is enforced rather than suggested. `ci-contract.ts` refuses a
# credential in a workflow-level or job-level `env:`, refuses one interpolated
# into a `run:` body, and requires an `if:` on any step that receives one; and
# `.codex/cloud/contract.toml`'s allow-list refuses any SENTRY_* entry outright,
# because cloud tasks never deploy, never push and never run host orchestration.
#
# The gate on the upload itself is intent TIMES credential, never an environment
# flag: a build with the token and no release name writes nothing. That is what
# stops a leaked CI token in a developer's shell from minting a release.
# capability:end sentry

# ── Project-specific (put these in secrets.d/<DEVCONTAINER_PROJECT>) ─────────
#
# Placeholders — replace with whatever this project actually needs. Keys here
# override the common tier, so a per-project value of a shared key is fine too.

# Database
# DATABASE_URL=

# Auth / session signing (generate: openssl rand -hex 32)
# AUTH_SECRET=

# Third-party service keys
# SOME_SERVICE_API_KEY=
